                      PASCAL SIMULATOR OPCODES

OVERVIEW

This file discusses the operation of, and opcodes for, the Pascal simulator
machine language. Passim takes any number of Pascal intermediate files,
optimized or unoptimized, and simulates them. To do this, we translate the
intermediate codes into an internal machine language, and run them on a
"reference" machine. This machine is designed to be optimal with Pascal
code structures.

IMPLEMENTATION

The central data structure for the reference machine is the memory array. This
is a large array of bytes, where the translated program, code, data and
variables are stored. All Pascal data types are stored in terms of bytes in the
array. This was deemed to be the most optimum "packing" method.
As running, the program has the following layout, which is very similar to
typical machine language implementations:                                 
                                                                          
     --------------------                                                 
     | Program code     |                                                 
     --------------------                                                 
     | Constants        |                                                 
     --------------------                                                 
     | Display          |                                                 
     --------------------                                                 
     | Global variables |                                                 
     --------------------                                                 
     | Heap             |                                                 
     |                  |                                                 
     ~                  ~                                                 
                                                                          
     ~                  ~                                                 
     |                  |                                                 
     | Stack            |                                                 
     --------------------                                                 
                                                                          
Where the heap grows up, and the stack grows down.                        
The program code starts at location 0, and each module read in is stacked in
the order specified in the command line. As in the real program, each module
performs startup/exit chaining with the next module, ending with the program
module.
After the program comes the fixed constants, the display, then the global
variables area. The heap is after that, and grows upwards.
All parameters are passed via the stack, and locals are stored there, too.
The format of a procedure/function frame is:

       ~                  ~
       | Function return  |
       --------------------
       | parameters       |
       |                  |
       --------------------                                                 
       | Return address   |                                                 
       --------------------                                                 
       | Locals           |
       |                  |                                                 
 disp->--------------------
       | Working stack    |
       ~                  ~
       ~                  ~
       |                  |
   sp->--------------------

All operations are resolved on the stack. Pushes onto the stack are performed
with 32 bits (4 bytes), which are pushed least significant to most significant.
Operands larger than 32 bits can be placed onto the stack, but they are only
poped by a type specific operator.
Structures are stored on the stack in their true length (which is probally
different from a true machine).
Parameters to procedures/functions are formed by simply leaving operands on
the stack. Because pushed items on the stack are in a univeral format, fetches
of parameters must fetch in that universal format.

FILES

Files a represented by a single byte file number. If the file has never been
opened before, the file nuber will be 0. This way, we can tell if the file has
never been accessed before, and assign a temp file to it.

TAGGED POINTERS

General arrays are indexed by tagged pointers. The format of a tagged pointer
is:

   0-3: Address
   4-7: Length

FORMAT OF INSTRUCTIONS

Each machine instruction consists of an instruction byte followed by some
number of parameters. All parameters are 32 bit.

OPERATORS

stkoff <num>            - Offset stack. Adds the immediate operand to the
                          stack pointer, which can be negative (to add stack
                          space) or positive (to remove it). The stack should
                          be checked for overflow, and may be checked for
                          underflow (as an internal verify check).

lodcloc <num>           - Load current local. Adds the immediate operand to the
                          stack pointer, and uses the resulting pointer to load
                          a 32 bit operand at that address. This is used to
                          load "universal" operands from the current local 
                          scope. The local beomes the new top of stack.

lodaloc <addr> <num>    - Load address local. Fetches the display pointer at
                          the first address, then adds the immediate offset to
                          that. The result is the address of a local in the
                          procedure/function of the display. The address
                          becomes the new top of stack.

rngchk <low> <high>     - Range check. Checks that the top of stack lies
                          between the two immediate operands. If not, this
                          should generate an error. The stack is unchanged.

indint                  - Load immediate integer. Loads the 32 bit integer
                          indirect from the address at stack top, and replaces
                          the stack top with that.

indrl                   - Load immediate real. Loads the 64 bit real indirect
                          from the address at stack top, and replaces the stack
                          top with that. Note that this creates a "fat" stack
                          entry.

indsrl                  - Load immediate short real. Loads the 32 bit real
                          indirect from the address at stack top, and replaces
                          the stack top with that. The short real is extended
                          to a 64 bit real on the stack. Note that this creates
                          a "fat" stack entry.

indset                  - Load immediate set. Loads the 256 bit (32 byte) set
                          indirect from the address at stack top, and replaces
                          the stack top with that. Note that this creates a 
                          "fat" stack entry.

indchr                  - Load immediate character. Loads the 8 bit character
                          indirect from the address at stack top, and replaces
                          the stack top with that. The character is zero
                          extended to be 32 bits. This operator is also used
                          to load booleans.

indstr <size>           - Load indirect structure. Loads the structure, whose
                          address is on the stack, and length is immediate,
                          onto the stack. Note that this creates a "fat" stack
                          entry.

lodiint <num>           - Load immediate integer. Loads the immediate integer
                          to the top of stack.

lodiirl <num>           - Load immediate real. Loads the immediate 64 bit real
                          to the top of stack. Note that this creates a "fat"
                          stack entry.

lodins                  - Load immediate null set. Places 32 bytes of zero onto
                          the stack (a "null" set). Note that this creates a 
                          "fat" stack entry.

notint                  - Not integer. Inverts all bits of the 32 bit operand
                          on the stack.

notbol                  - Not boolean. Expects a 32 boolean on the stack. If
                          the value is 0, it is replaced with 1, else it is
                          replaced with 0.

setsin                  - Set single element. Expects a 32 bit integer on the
                          stack top, and a 32 byte set below that. The bit
                          corresponding to the integer is set. If the bit is
                          beyond 255, an error is processed. The integer is
                          removed.

setrng                  - Set range of elements. Expects a two 32 bit integers
                          on the stack, with a 32 byte set below that. All the
                          bits between the second value on the stack and the
                          top value are set in the set. The two values are
                          removed.

cvtitr                  - Convert integer to real. Converts the 32 bit integer
                          on the stack to a 64 bit real on the stack. Note that
                          this creates a "fat" stack entry.

cvtfix <size>           - Convert tagged pointer to fixed. Converts the tagged
                          pointer on the stack to fixed by checking that the
                          length of the object matches the immediate operand,
                          then discarding the length portion of the pointer to
                          leave just the address.

cvttag <size>           - Convert fixed pointer to tagged. Contains the size of
                          the fixed array. Expects a fixed pointer on the
                          stack. The fixed pointer is replaced by a tagged
                          pointer using the address of the fixed and the length
                          provided.

setint                  - Find set intersection. Expects two sets on the stack.
                          Finds the intersection between them, or the 'and' of
                          all the bits between the sets. The result replaces
                          both sets on the stack.

mltrl                   - Multiply real. Expects two 64 bit reals on the stack.
                          The reals are multiplied, and the result replaces
                          them both on the stack.

mltint                  - Multiply integer. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The integers are multipled, and the result
                          replaces them both on the stack.

divrl                   - Divide real. Expects two 64 bit reals on the stack.
                          The second real on the stack is divided by the top
                          real on the stack, and the result replaces them both
                          on the stack.

divint                  - Divide integer. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The second integer on the stack is divided by
                          the top integer on the stack, and the result replaces
                          them both on the stack.

modint                  - Modulo integer. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The second integer on the stack is divided by
                          the top integer on the stack, and the modulo of that
                          division replaces them both on the stack.

andint                  - 'and' integers. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The two integers are 'and'ed, and the result
                          replaces them both.

negint                  - Negate integer. Negates the 32 bit integer on the
                          stack.

negrl                   - Negates the 64 bit real on the stack.

setuni                  - Set union. Expects two 32 byte sets on the stack.
                          The union of the sets is found, or the 'or' of all
                          set bits. The result replaces them both on the stack.

addrl                   - Add real. Expects two 64 bit reals on the stack. The
                          reals are added, and the result replaces them both
                          on the stack.

addint                  - Add integer. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The integers are added, and the result 
                          replaces them both on the stack.

setdif                  - Set difference. Expects two 32 byte sets on the
                          stack. Finds the difference between the sets, or
                          (second xor top) and second.

subrl                   - Subtract real. Expects two 64 bit reals on the stack.
                          Subtracts the top real from the second real, and the
                          result replaces them both on the stack.

subint                  - Subtract integer. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. Subtracts the top integer from the second
                          integer, and the result replaces them both on the
                          stack.

orint                   - 'or' integers. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The two integers are 'or'ed, and the result
                          replaces them both.

xorint                  - 'xor' integers. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The two integers are 'xor'ed, and the result
                          replaces them both.

setin                   - Set inclusion. Expects a 32 byte set on the stack,
                          and a 32 bit value below that. A test is made if the
                          bit by the value exists in the set, and a 32 bit
                          boolean 0 or 1 truth value replaces them both.

equset                  - Set equal. Expects two 32 byte sets on the stack. The
                          sets are compared for equality, and a 32 bit boolean
                          value replaces them both.

equrl                   - Real equal. Expects two 64 bit reals on the stack. 
                          The reals are compared for equality, and a 32 bit 
                          boolean value replaces them both.

equstr <size>           - String equal. Contains the 32 bit size of the
                          strings. Expects two 32 bit pointers to strings on
                          the stack. The strings are compared for equality, and
                          a 32 bit boolean value replaces both strings.

equgst                  - General string equal. Expects two tagged pointers to 
                          strings on the stack. If the lengths of the tags do
                          not match, an error occurs. The strings are compared
                          for equality, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces
                          both strings.

equint                  - Integer equal. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The integers are compared for equality, and a
                          32 bit boolean value replaces them both.

ltnrl                   - Real less than. Expects two 64 bit reals on the 
                          stack. The reals are compared for second less than
                          top, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces them both.

ltnstr <size>           - String less than. Contains the 32 bit size of the
                          strings. Expects two 32 bit pointers to strings on
                          the stack. The strings are compared for second less
                          than top, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces both
                          strings.

ltngst                  - General string less than. Expects two tagged pointers
                          to strings on the stack. If the lengths of the tags
                          do not match, an error occurs. The strings are
                          compared for second less than top, and a 32 bit
                          boolean value replaces both strings.

ltnint                  - Integer less than. Expects two 32 bit integers on the
                          stack. The integers are compared for second less than
                          top, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces them both.

gtnrl                   - Real greater than. Expects two 64 bit reals on the 
                          stack. The reals are compared for second greater than
                          top, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces them both.

gtnstr <size>           - String greater than. Contains the 32 bit size of the
                          strings. Expects two 32 bit pointers to strings on
                          the stack. The strings are compared for second 
                          greater than top, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces
                          both strings.

gtngst                  - General string greater than. Expects two tagged
                          pointers to strings on the stack. If the lengths of
                          the tags do not match, an error occurs. The strings
                          are compared for second greater than top, and a 32
                          bit boolean value replaces both strings.

gtnint                  - Integer greater than. Expects two 32 bit integers on
                          the stack. The integers are compared for second
                          greater than top, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces
                          them both.

leqset                  - Set less than or equal. Expects two 32 byte sets on 
                          the stack. Checks if all the elements of set second
                          are included in the top, or (second and top) = 
                          second. A a 32 bit boolean value replaces them both.

geqset                  - Set greater than or equal. Expects two 32 byte sets
                          on the stack. Checks if all the elements of set top
                          are included in the top, or (second and top) = top. A
                          a 32 bit boolean value replaces them both.

jmp <num>               - Jump unconditional. Contains the address of the
                          branch location. Begins execution at the location.

jpf <num>               - Jump false. Contains the address of the branch
                          location. Expects a 32 bit boolean value on the
                          stack. If the boolean = 0, then the branch is taken.

jpt <num>               - Jump true. Contains the address of the branch
                          location. Expects a 32 bit boolean value on the
                          stack. If the boolean <> 0, then the branch is taken.

duptop                  - Duplicate top of stack. The 32 bit operand at stack
                          top is duplicated. Must not be used with "fat"
                          operands.

poptop                  - Remove top of stack. The 32 bit operand at stack top
                          is discarded. Must not be used with "fat" operands.

swprr                   - Swap top of stack. Swaps the top 64 bit operand on
                          the stack with the 64 bit second on the stack.

swpri                   - Swap top of stack. Swaps the top 32 bit operand on
                          the stack with the 64 bit second on the stack.

swpir                   - Swap top of stack. Swaps the top 64 bit operand on
                          the stack with the 32 bit second on the stack.

swpii                   - Swap top of stack. Swaps the top 32 bit operand on
                          the stack with the 32 bit second on the stack.

swpti                   - Swap top of stack. Swaps the top 32 bit operand on
                          the stack with the 64 bit tagged pointer second on
                          the stack.

sucint                  - Increment integer. Increments the 32 bit integer at
                          stack top.

prdint                  - Decrement integer. Decrements the 32 bit integer at
                          stack top.

goto <addr> <addr>      - Goto procedural. Contains the address of the target
                          procedure/function display, and the address of the
                          target code. The stack pointer receives the address
                          of the display, and execution continues at the
                          specified point.

call <addr>             - Call procedural. Contains the address of the target
                          procedure/function. The address of the NEXT
                          instruction is saved on the stack, and execution
                          continues at the specified address.

calli                   - Call procedural indirect. Expects the address of the
                          target procedure/function as tos. The address of the
                          NEXT instruction is saved on the stack, and execution
                          continues at the specified address. This instruction
                          is used for procedure/function parameters.

ret                     - Return. The address on the stack is set as the
                          execution address. The address is removed from the
                          stack.

retoff <off>            - Return and offset stack. Contains a signed offset.
                          The return address on the stack is saved, the offset
                          is added to the stack, and execution continues at
                          the return address. Used to remove parameters from
                          the stack on return.

wrtfil <size>           - Write file. Contains the size of the object to be
                          written (in bytes). Expects a pointer to the object
                          on the stack, and a pointer to the file below that.
                          The bytes specified are written to the file, and
                          the object pointer removed from the stack. The file
                          is left on the stack.

wrtbol                  - Convert and write boolean to file. Expects a 32 bit
                          boolean on stack top, and under that a pointer to a
                          file. The boolean is converted to "true"/"false"
                          string, and that is output. The boolean is removed,
                          but the file left on stack.

wrtgst                  - Write general string to file. Expects a tagged
                          pointer to the general string on stack top, and under
                          that a pointer to a file. The string is output to the
                          file. The pointer is removed, but the file is left on
                          stack.

wrtintf                 - Convert and write integer to file with field. Expects
                          A 32 bit field, and under that a 32 bit integer, and
                          under that a pointer to a file. The integer is
                          converted to ascii form and output using the 
                          specified field. The field and integer are removed,
                          but the file is left on stack.

wrtchrf                 - Write character to file with field. Expects A 32 bit
                          field, and under that a 32 bit character, and under
                          that a pointer to a file. The character is output 
                          using the specified field. The character and field
                          are removed, but the file is left on stack.

wrtbolf                 - Write boolean to file with field. Expects A 32 bit
                          field, and under that a 32 bit boolean, and under
                          that a pointer to a file. The boolean is converted
                          to "true"/"false", and that string output using the
                          field. The boolean and field are removed, but the
                          file is left on stack.

wrtrlf                  - Write real to file with field. Expects A 32 bit
                          field, and under that a 64 bit real, and under
                          that a pointer to a file. The real is output in fixed
                          format using the specified field. The real and field
                          are removed, but the file is left on stack.

wrtstrf <len>           - Write string to file with field. Contains the length
                          of the string to be written. Expects A 32 bit field,
                          and under that a 32 pointer to a string, followed by
                          the file pointer. The string is output using the
                          field. The field and string are removed, but the file
                          is left on the stack.

wrtgstf                 - Write general string to file with field. Expects a
                          32 bit field, and under that a tagged pointer to a
                          general string, and under that a file pointer. The
                          string is output using the field. The field and
                          string are removed, but the file is left on the
                          stack.

wrtrlff                 - Write real to file with field and fraction. Expects
                          A 32 bit fraction on the stack top, followed by a 32
                          bit field, followed by a 64 bit real, followed by the
                          file pointer. The real is written in floating point
                          format, using the field and fraction. The field,
                          fraction and real are removed, but the file pointer
                          is left on the stack.

wrtfsrl                 - Write short real direct to file. Expects a 64 bit
                          real on stack top, followed by a file pointer. A
                          short real is written to the file in binary. The
                          short real is removed, but the file is left on stack.

wrtfrl                  - Write real direct to file. Expects a 64 bit real on
                          stack top, followed by a file pointer. The real is
                          written to the file in binary. The real is removed,
                          but the file is left on stack.

wrtfset                 - Write set direct to file. Expects a 32 byte set on
                          stack top, followed by a file pointer. The set is
                          written to the file in binary. The set is removed,
                          but the file is left on stack.

wrtfchr                 - Write character direct to file. Expects a 32 bit
                          character on stack top, followed by a file pointer.
                          The character is written to the file in binary (one
                          byte only). The character is removed,but the file is
                          left on stack. Note that this is used to write
                          booleans as well.

wrtfint                 - Write integer direct to file. Expects a 32 bit
                          integer on stack top, followed by a file pointer. The
                          set is written to the file in binary. The set is 
                          removed, but the file is left on stack.

wrteoln                 - Write end of line to file. Expects a file pointer on
                          the stack top. The end of line sequence is written to
                          the file. The file is left on stack.

rdfil <size>            - Read file structure. Contains the byte size of the
                          operand to be written. Expects the address of the
                          structure to be read, followed by a pointer to the
                          file on the stack. The bytes in the structure are
                          read from the file. The structure pointer is removed,
                          but the file is left on stack.

rdint                   - Read and convert integer from file. Expects a pointer
                          to the integer on stack top, and a pointer to the
                          file under that. Any blanks or newlines in the file
                          are skipped, and an integer, if it exists, read and
                          converted from ascii to binary form, then stored to
                          the result. The integer pointer is removed from the
                          stack, but the file is left.

rdchr                   - Read character from file. Expects a pointer to the
                          character on stack top, and a pointer to the file
                          under that. The next character is read from the file
                          and stored at the result. If the next character is
                          the end of line, a blank is returned. The character
                          pointer is removed from the stack, but the file is
                          left.

rdrl                    - Read and convert real from file. Expects a pointer
                          to the real on stack top, and a pointer to the
                          file under that. Any blanks or newlines in the file
                          are skipped, and a real, if it exists, read and
                          converted from ascii to binary form, then stored to
                          the result. The real pointer is removed from the
                          stack, but the file is left.

rdsrl                   - Read and convert short real from file. Expects a
                          pointer to the short real on stack top, and a pointer
                          to the file under that. Any blanks or newlines in the
                          file are skipped, and a short real, if it exists,
                          read and converted from ascii to binary form, then
                          stored to the result. The short real pointer is
                          removed from the stack, but the file is left.

rdeoln                  - Read eoln from file. Expects a pointer to the file
                          on stack. Any characters are skipped up to and
                          including the eoln. The file is left on stack.

absrl                   - Find absolute real. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. Finds the absolute value of the real, and
                          that replaces the real on stack.

absint                  - Find absolute integer. Expects a 32 bit integer on
                          the stack. Finds the absolute value of the integer,
                          and that replaces the integer on stack.

sqrrl                   - Find square of real. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. Finds the square of the real, and that 
                          replaces the real on stack.

sqrint                  - Find square of integer. Expects a 32 bit integer on
                          the stack. Finds the square of the integer, and that
                          replaces the integer on stack.

atnrl                   - Find arctan of real. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. Finds the arctan of the real, and that 
                          replaces the real on stack.

cosrl                   - Find cosine of real. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. Finds the cosine of the real, and that 
                          replaces the real on stack.

exprl                   - Find exponent of real. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. Finds the exponent of the real, and that 
                          replaces the real on stack.

lnrl                    - Find natural log of real. Expects a 64 bit real on
                          the stack. Finds the natural log of the real, and
                          that replaces the real on stack.

sinrl                   - Find sine of real. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. Finds the sine of the real, and that 
                          replaces the real on stack.

sqtrl                   - Find square root of real. Expects a 64 bit real on
                          the stack. Finds the square root of the real, and
                          that replaces the real on stack.

eoln                    - Check eoln of file. Expects a 32 bit pointer to a
                          file on stack. A boolean 32 bit representing the
                          eoln status of the file replaces the file.

eof                     - Check eof of file. Expects a 32 bit pointer to a
                          file on stack. A boolean 32 bit representing the
                          eof status of the file replaces the file.

odd                     - Check integer odd. Expects a 32 bit integer on stack
                          top. Replaces that with a 32 bit boolean that is true
                          if the integer is odd, ie., clears all bits in the
                          integer but bit 0.

rnd                     - Round to integer. Expects a 64 bit real on the stack.
                          The real is converted to integer by rounding, and
                          replaces the real on the stack.

trc                     - Truncate to integer. Expects a 64 bit real on the
                          stack. The real is converted to integer by
                          truncation, and replaces the real on the stack.

exist                   - Check file existance. Expects a tagged pointer to 
                          the file string on stack. The file is checked for 
                          existance, and a 32 bit boolean value replaces the
                          string on stack.

len                     - Check file length. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on
                          stack. Replaces that with the 32 bit length of the
                          file.

loc                     - Check file location. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on
                          stack. Replaces that with the 32 bit location of the
                          file, which will be 1 - length.

get                     - Get file next. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on the
                          stack. The next element in the file is set active.
                          Removes the file pointer.

gett                    - Get text file next. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on
                          the stack. The next element in the file is set 
                          active. Removes the file pointer.
                          This call is the same as for get, but allows special
                          processing to be performed on a text file.

put                     - Put file next. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on the
                          stack. Writes the contents of the file buffer
                          variable to the file. Removes the file pointer.

lodafbuf                - Load address of file buffer. Expects a 32 bit file
                          pointer on the stack. Loads the address of the file's
                          buffer variable, and that replaces the file pointer
                          on stack. This is also an opportunity to process
                          "lazy I/O" on a read mode file.

lodafbuft               - Load address of text file buffer. Expects a 32 bit
                          file pointer on the stack. Loads the address of the
                          file's buffer variable, and that replaces the file
                          pointer on stack. This is also an opportunity to
                          process "lazy I/O" on a read mode file.
                          This call is the same as for lodafbuf, but allows
                          special processing to be performed on a text file.

reset <size>            - Reset file. Contains the record size for the file.
                          Expects a 32 bit file pointer on the stack. If the
                          file has never been accessed before (the pointer is
                          0), then the file is given a temp name, otherwise the
                          previous assign name is used. The record size is
                          given to the file. The file is opened in read mode.
                          The file pointer is removed.

resett <size>           - Identical to reset, except that the use of a text
                          file is denoted. This allows the creation of a text
                          only mode file.

rewrite <size>          - Rewrite file. Contains the record size for the file.
                          Expects a 32 bit file pointer on the stack. If the
                          file has never been accessed before (the pointer is
                          0), then the file is given a temp name, otherwise the
                          previous assign name is used. The record size is
                          given to the file. The file is opened in write mode,
                          and any contents cleared. The file pointer is
                          removed.

rewritet <size>         - Identical to rewrite, except that the use of a text
                          file is denoted. This allows the creation of a text
                          only mode file.

close                   - Close file. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on the
                          stack. The file is closed. The file is removed from
                          the stack.

page                    - Page file. Expects a 32 bit file pointer on the
                          stack. A form feed is sent to the file. The file is
                          removed from the stack.

assign                  - Assign name to file. Expects a tagged pointer to a
                          string on stack top, followed by a 32 bit file
                          pointer below that. The name is given to the file,
                          which must not be open or previously assigned. The
                          string and file pointers are removed.

pos                     - Position file. Expects the 32 bit position (1-N) on
                          the stack, followed by a 32 bit file pointer. The
                          file is positioned. Both the position and the file
                          are removed.

del                     - Delete file. Contains the size of a string. Expects
                          a tagged string pointer on stack. The file by that
                          name is removed. The string pointer is removed from
                          the stack.

chg                     - Change filename. Expects a tagged pointer to the
                          source name on stack top, followed by a tagged
                          pointer to the destination name. The file by the
                          name of the source is changed to the destination name.

stosrl                  - Store short real. Expects a 32 bit short real on
                          stack, followed by a 32 bit variable address. The
                          short real is stored to the address. Both operands
                          are removed.

stosrl                  - Store short real. Expects a 32 bit short real on
                          stack, followed by a 32 bit variable address. The
                          short real is stored to the address. Both operands
                          are removed.

storl                   - Store real. Expects a 64 bit real on stack, followed
                          by a 32 bit variable address. The real is stored to
                          the address. Both operands are removed.

stoi                    - Store integer. Expects a 32 bit integer on stack, 
                          followed by a 32 bit variable address. The integer
                          is stored to the address. Both operands are removed.

stochr                  - Store character. Expects a 32 bit character on stack, 
                          followed by a 32 bit variable address. The character
                          is stored to the address. Both operands are removed.

stoset                  - Store set. Expects a 32 byte set on stack, followed
                          by a 32 bit variable address. The set is stored to
                          the address. Both operands are removed.

stostr <size>           - Store structure. Contains the structure size. Expects
                          a pointer to a structure on stack, followed by a 32
                          bit variable address. The structure is copied to the
                          address. Both operands are removed.

stogar <size>           - Store general array. Contains the size of the base
                          array element. Expects the source array as a tagged
                          pointer at stack top, and the destination array as
                          a tagged pointer under that. If the array sizes do
                          not match, an error is indicated. All of the elements
                          in the source are copied to the destination.

stotgp                  - Store tagged pointer. Expects a tagged pointer at
                          stack top, and a 32 bit pointer to the destination
                          under that. The tagged pointer is placed at the
                          destination address.

stostk <addr>           - Store stack pointer. Contains the address to store
                          the stack to. The current stack pointer is stored
                          to the address given. The stack is unchanged.

new <size>              - Allocate dynamic variable. Contains the variable
                          size. Expects the 32 bit address of a pointer
                          variable on stack. A variable of the given size is
                          allocated, and the address placed at the address.
                          The variable address is removed.

disp <size>             - Free dynamic variable. Contains the variable
                          size. Expects the 32 bit pointer to the variable
                          on stack. The variable is freed. The variable is
                          removed.


newgar <size>           - Allocate general array. Contains the size of the base
                          element. Expects the number of elements on stack top,
                          followed by a (simple) pointer to the location of a
                          tagged pointer to be allocated. The number of
                          elements is verified to lie within 1..maxint, then
                          elements*size bytes allocated. The address of the
                          tagged pointer is set to the base allocation, and
                          the length receives the number of elements.
                          The length and pointer are removed from the stack.
                          
dspgar <size>           - Deallocate general array. Contains the size of the
                          base element. Expects a tagged pointer on stack top.
                          The object indexed by the pointer is freed. The size
                          of the object can be found by elements*size. The
                          tagged pointer is removed from the stack.

arfgar <size>           - General array reference. Contains the size of the
                          general array base element. Expects the index for the
                          array on stack top, followed by the tagged pointer to
                          the general array. The index is verified to lie
                          within 1 and the tag length, and an error results if
                          not. Then, the index is adjusted to 0, multiplied by
                          the base element size, and offset by the array base.
                          This element address then replaces both operands on
                          the stack.

exit                    - Exit simulation. Causes the simulation to halt.
